<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Merania</h1><p class="st-meta">1152 CE – 1248 CE · capital Andechs</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A title duchy of the Andechs counts on the Adriatic coast, whose family married into the royal houses of France, Hungary and Poland before dying out in 1248.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1152 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a> gave Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conrad-ii-count-of-dachau">Conrad of Dachau</a> the title Duke of Merania, a coastal area on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kvarner-gulf">Kvarner Gulf</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/histria">Istria</a>. After the Dachau line died out, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-andechs">Andechs</a> counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> took the title in 1180, holding lands in Bavaria, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a>, Istria and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-burgundy">Burgundy</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The duchy was more a title than a territory, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-andechs">Andechs-Merania</a> dukes were among the leading princes of the Empire. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berthold-iv-duke-of-merania">Berthold IV</a>'s daughters married widely: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agnes-of-merania">Agnes</a> became Queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gertrude-of-merania">Gertrude</a> Queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-of-hungary">Saint Elizabeth</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hedwig-of-silesia">Hedwig</a> Duchess of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-silesia">Silesia</a>, later Saint Hedwig.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The family lost lands after allegedly being involved in the murder of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-of-swabia">Philip of Swabia</a> in 1208. The last duke, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-ii-duke-of-merania">Otto II</a>, died in 1248, and the lands were divided among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach">Wittelsbachs</a>, the counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> and others. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-andechs">Andechs</a> became a monastery.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-merania.jpg" alt="Duchy of Merania" width="320" height="195"><figcaption>Image: anonymous · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Andechsove.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1152 CE – 1248 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Andechs</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Conrad of Dachau, Berthold IV of Andechs, Otto I of Merania</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bavarian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/croatia">Croatia</a>, <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>, <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Meran, Andechs-Merania</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1200&amp;focus=duchy-of-merania" aria-label="Duchy of Merania on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4940 498 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Merania lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5260 754L5229 694"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5260 754h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5260 754h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Andechs. <a href="/atlas?year=1200&amp;focus=duchy-of-merania">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

